g the desired final microstructure. [Data: Entities (937); Relationships (1261, 1309)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Transition-temperature prediction extends beyond grain size\",\"explanation\":\"The Gladman model for bainitic steel incorporates silicon, free nitrogen, strengthening contributions, and grain size into a transition-temperature equation. Silicon is separately identified as a strong ferrite former and as a contributor to transition temperature. [Data: Entities (817, 820, 806); Relationships (1055, 1453)]\\n\\nOther models address specialized structures: the Kunitake model treats the martensitic facet unit as the effective grain size, while martensitic blocks interrupt crack propagation in a manner analogous to large-angle grains. These models show that transition behavior depends on phase morphology and strengthening contributions as well as conventional grain size. [Data: Entities (779, 816, 819, 794); Relationships (1050, 1020, 1037)]\"},{\"summary\":\"The Bauschinger effect is an important qualification to strength interpretation\",\"explanation\":\"The Bauschinger effect is identified as a cause of yield-strength loss and as having a pronounced influence on discontinuous yielding in polygonal steels. This provides a countervailing mechanism to the strengthening effects of grain refinement, precipitation, and deformation. [Data: Entities (1016, 1025, 1026); Relationships (1392, 1442, 1443)]\\n\\nFor acicular ferrite steel, the supplied description states that the loss in yield strength is offset by continuous yielding followed by rapid work hardening. Therefore, material structure and yielding mode must be considered when interpreting process-property relationships. [Data: Entities (536, 1016)]\"}],\"rating\":7.0,\"rating_explanation\":\"The community merits a high impact rating because it integrates industrial steel-processing methods, predictive models, microstructural controls, and mechanical-performance outcomes, although the data show no evidence of legal,